WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.994 --> 00:00:03.281
A unit test is easy.

2
00:00:03.321 --> 00:00:06.433
One input, one answer; and
the same answer every time.

3
00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:08.109
Now try testing a conversation.

4
00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:10.365
On the right of the screen,
the interview branches for each

5
00:00:10.405 --> 00:00:11.018
turn.

6
00:00:11.058 --> 00:00:13.400
When you run it again, a
different branch occurs.

7
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.161
There is no single assert to check.

8
00:00:15.201 --> 00:00:16.204
So how did we check it?

9
00:00:16.244 --> 00:00:17.321
One case at a time.

10
00:00:17.361 --> 00:00:20.979
Type an opening into Postman,
read the reply, type the next.

11
00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:23.962
Or deploy it all the way to PPE
and test the larger interface

12
00:00:24.002 --> 00:00:24.784
by hand.

13
00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:27.367
It was so slow that customers were
finding the regressions before

14
00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:28.944
we did.

15
00:00:29.244 --> 00:00:31.509
Let's look at one specific
scenario definition.

16
00:00:31.549 --> 00:00:34.181
The opening is what the conversation
starts with: my email has

17
00:00:34.221 --> 00:00:35.345
just stopped working.

18
00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:37.923
That is deliberately vague,
because that is how people actually

19
00:00:37.963 --> 00:00:38.573
write.

20
00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:42.796
Underneath it sits the target:
Outlook, on iOS, not receiving email.

21
00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:45.604
The interview never sees that,
we are using it to grade once

22
00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:46.977
the conversation ends.

23
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:48.402
Then we have the knowledge bank.

24
00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:51.567
Three authored facts this customer
knows and will say if asked:

25
00:00:51.607 --> 00:00:54.009
that they are using the mail
app on an iPhone; that they can

26
00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:57.236
still send messages, but nothing
new ever arrives; and that they

27
00:00:57.276 --> 00:00:59.709
have checked junk, and
the mailbox is not full.

28
00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:00.800
Notice the second one.

29
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.561
Can send, cannot receive — that is the
detail separating this from a dozen

30
00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:07.202
neighbouring categories, and
the interview only learns it if

31
00:01:07.242 --> 00:01:08.345
it thinks to ask.

32
00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:11.205
Then a pool of alternative
phrasings, so the customer can choose

33
00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:13.637
different phrasings to
not repeat every time.

34
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:16.749
And last, the assertions
this scenario has to satisfy.

35
00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:19.233
That is what makes it a
conversation and not a prompt.

36
00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:22.128
We have defined nearly
two-hundred of them.

37
00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:25.155
But here is the hard part: the
thing being tested is a language

38
00:01:25.195 --> 00:01:25.795
model.

39
00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:29.244
It writes its own questions, and it
will ask things nobody anticipated.

40
00:01:29.284 --> 00:01:32.210
A script of canned answers can
fall apart on the first unexpected

41
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:32.828
turn.

42
00:01:32.868 --> 00:01:36.193
So the simulated customer answers
by rule, in priority order.

43
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:39.139
If the question offers choices,
and those choices carry routing

44
00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:42.692
data, it picks the one whose route
runs closest to its own target.

45
00:01:42.732 --> 00:01:45.314
If the choices are platforms,
it answers with the platform it

46
00:01:45.354 --> 00:01:46.373
is actually on.

47
00:01:46.413 --> 00:01:49.611
If it is any other multiple choice,
it scores each option against

48
00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:50.859
its own vocabulary.

49
00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:53.804
And if the question has no
choices at all — free text,

50
00:01:53.844 --> 00:01:56.918
invented on the spot — it draws
the next unused line from its

51
00:01:56.958 --> 00:01:57.924
knowledge bank.

52
00:01:57.964 --> 00:01:59.780
One guard matters more than the rest.

53
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.222
The customer will never name
a platform it is not on.

54
00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:04.423
Those phrasings come
from real support data.

55
00:02:04.463 --> 00:02:07.537
Matching is on word boundaries
— which is why Xbox Game Studios

56
00:02:07.577 --> 00:02:10.056
is not an iOS case.

57
00:02:10.356 --> 00:02:13.286
Each of the hundreds of scenarios
opens its own conversation

58
00:02:13.326 --> 00:02:16.825
and shares no state with any other,
so they can go out in parallel.

59
00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:20.659
And we run every scenario three times,
because the same input doesn't give

60
00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:22.111
the same answer twice.

61
00:02:22.151 --> 00:02:25.313
On screen, three result strips
for one scenario land on three

62
00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:28.272
different outputs, and an average
line resolves between them.

63
00:02:28.312 --> 00:02:32.464
Replication across this suite showed the
same scenario changing grade from one run

64
00:02:32.504 --> 00:02:34.539
to the next, for no reason at all.

65
00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:37.708
So we average across three runs,
and a real change is separated

66
00:02:37.748 --> 00:02:40.128
from the model's own noise.

67
00:02:40.428 --> 00:02:41.870
And it is not grading one thing.

68
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.064
Did the interview reach the right
category — sixty-seven scenarios

69
00:02:45.104 --> 00:02:45.810
on that.

70
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.857
Did it ask for the context it
needed — fifty more, drawn from

71
00:02:48.897 --> 00:02:50.244
real customer queries.

72
00:02:50.284 --> 00:02:53.534
Does it repeat itself or leak
across products — forty-five.

73
00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:56.129
And the sharpest one: does
it refuse what it should,

74
00:02:56.169 --> 00:02:57.726
without refusing what it shouldn't?

75
00:02:57.766 --> 00:03:01.014
A customer opens about Outlook,
then asks how to order a pizza.

76
00:03:01.054 --> 00:03:03.326
The interview must
notice and clarify scope.

77
00:03:03.366 --> 00:03:06.962
But it must not refuse ordinary
requests that merely sound odd — someone

78
00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:08.538
overriding an inbox rule.

79
00:03:08.578 --> 00:03:11.480
Across all hundred and sixty-two
scenarios: around a hundred

80
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.776
and thirty-five must not refuse, a
handful must, and zero counted in both.

81
00:03:17.088 --> 00:03:19.926
Two harnesses, and the lanes
on screen fill against the same

82
00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:20.709
clock.

83
00:03:20.749 --> 00:03:25.027
The upper lane, labelled U I, clears
thirteen scenarios in three runs each

84
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:26.932
— about three and a half minutes.

85
00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:31.600
The lower lane, labelled A P I, clears all
hundred and sixty-two in less time than

86
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:32.224
that.

87
00:03:32.264 --> 00:03:35.389
So the fast tier is the pull-request
gate; the interface harness

88
00:03:35.429 --> 00:03:38.544
cannot target a pull-request build at all.

89
00:03:38.844 --> 00:03:41.341
But the slow tier sees something
the fast one is structurally

90
00:03:41.381 --> 00:03:42.198
blind to.

91
00:03:42.238 --> 00:03:45.189
Watch it drive the real U I,
and a stage trace surfaces:

92
00:03:45.229 --> 00:03:49.090
running Outlook Q and A agent,
agent completed, workflow failed.

93
00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.113
The interview got the category
right, the right agent was picked,

94
00:03:52.153 --> 00:03:53.752
and resolution failed anyway.

95
00:03:53.792 --> 00:03:55.411
The customer got a canned refusal.

96
00:03:55.451 --> 00:03:58.561
The fast tier stops at the category
and cannot say any of that.

97
00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:01.440
Different data, not just slower data.

98
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.507
We deploy the pull-request
branch, point the harness at it,

99
00:04:04.547 --> 00:04:05.925
and run it three times.

100
00:04:05.965 --> 00:04:08.531
Then the same suite against
main, and we diff the two.

101
00:04:08.571 --> 00:04:10.917
The comparison exists
before the review does.

102
00:04:10.957 --> 00:04:13.492
On one real change,
routing got clearly better.

103
00:04:13.532 --> 00:04:16.015
Cases reaching the right support
area went from twenty-seven

104
00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.127
to thirty-five out of sixty-seven.

105
00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:20.617
Cases where the interview came
back with nothing were almost

106
00:04:20.657 --> 00:04:21.333
halved.

107
00:04:21.373 --> 00:04:23.444
Twenty-six fewer failed assertions.

108
00:04:23.484 --> 00:04:27.418
And when something regresses, we see
it immediately — in the same report,

109
00:04:27.458 --> 00:04:30.308
on the same run, while the
change is still a branch.

110
00:04:30.348 --> 00:04:31.296
Not in production.

111
00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:32.179
Not in review.

112
00:04:32.219 --> 00:04:34.071
Before the pull request was ever opened.

113
00:04:34.111 --> 00:04:35.258
That is what this buys.

114
00:04:35.298 --> 00:04:36.528
Run it against your branch first.
